      "id": "delimiter-eof-unread",
      "text": "Start with NewBufferString(\"ab\") and call ReadString('\\n'). It returns \"ab\" and io.EOF, advances to the end, and records a read. Call ReadString('\\n') again. It returns an empty string and io.EOF and still records a read. UnreadByte then succeeds and the next ReadByte returns 'b'. ReadBytes follows the same state transitions. On a fresh empty buffer, either delimiter read followed by UnreadByte also reports success, with no byte restored. As a control, an empty ReadByte resets the read state and the following UnreadByte returns an error. The delimiter cases conflict with the UnreadByte comment for reads that return an error or consume zero bytes.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 466,
        "fragment": "b.lastRead = opRead"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Should delimiter reads that return io.EOF invalidate UnreadByte, or should the contract describe when an earlier byte can be replayed?"
    },
    {
      "id": "grow-between-read-and-unread",
      "text": "Start with a buffer whose backing slice has length and capacity 8 and contains \"abcdefgh\". Read seven bytes, then call Grow(3). There is no tail room, and the one unread byte plus the requested three bytes fit in half the capacity, so growth slides 'h' to index zero and preserves the last-read marker. UnreadByte returns nil with the offset already at zero, and ReadByte returns 'h'. In the same initial state, UnreadByte immediately after the seven-byte read restores 'g'. Grow(0) between the read and UnreadByte also preserves 'g' because that call takes the reslice path. If the preceding read consumes all eight bytes, even Grow(0) resets the buffer and UnreadByte returns an error.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 172,
        "fragment": "b.off = 0"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: What should UnreadByte guarantee across Grow when growth can preserve the read marker while discarding the byte it refers to?"
    },
    {
      "id": "write-to-report-before-reset",
      "text": "Start with unread data and a writer whose reported count is within the offered slice length. WriteTo calls Write once and advances by the reported count before inspecting the error. A writer reporting a partial count and an error returns that same error and leaves the unreported suffix for a later call. A partial count with nil returns io.ErrShortWrite and leaves the same suffix. A full count with nil drains and resets the buffer. A full count with an error drains it and returns the original error before reset, so Len is zero but the consumed prefix still reduces Available. A subsequent WriteTo skips the writer and resets the buffer, reclaiming the retained capacity. Outside the writer contract, an over-count panics before offset advancement, while a negative count with an initial offset of zero can leave a negative offset.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 286,
        "fragment": "return n, e"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Should a full count accompanied by an error reclaim the consumed prefix immediately or leave that cleanup to a later operation?"
    },
    {
      "id": "read-from-commit-and-retry",
      "text": "Start with a valid buffer and a reader that leaves the buffer itself alone. Each ReadFrom iteration reserves at least 512 bytes of writable space, restores the current logical length, and calls the reader with the available tail. A valid positive count is appended and included in the total before the error is checked. Exact io.EOF becomes nil, while any other non-nil error, including a wrapper around io.EOF, is returned unchanged. A (0, nil) result starts another iteration with no retry limit in this loop. If the reader later panics or returns a negative count, the call exits abruptly and earlier committed bytes remain readable. The failing iteration has already run growth, so capacity or layout may have changed.",
      "citation": {
