    },
    {
      "id": "copy-and-storage-ownership",
      "text": "Copy a zero-value Buffer and write a nonempty string to each copy. Each acquires its own storage. Instead, start with a Buffer containing \"ab\" and copy the Buffer value. ReadByte on one copy advances only its offset, and ReadByte on the other still returns 'a'. Both copies still share the backing array. Obtain Bytes from the copy with unread 'b', then reset the original and write \"xy\" into its retained storage. The copy's next ReadByte returns 'y'. Rebinding a caller's pointer to a different Buffer leaves another pointer to the original value usable, while assigning a new Buffer value through the caller's existing pointer changes what both pointers see. NewBuffer also uses the supplied slice directly and documents transfer of ownership to the buffer.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 25,
        "fragment": "Copying and modifying a non-zero Buffer is prone to error,"
      },
      "question": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "peek-next-and-unread",
      "text": "Start with NewBufferString(\"ab\") and read 'a' with ReadByte. Peek(2) returns the remaining \"b\" and io.EOF, leaves Len at 1, and preserves the read marker, so UnreadByte restores 'a'. From the same starting state after ReadByte, Next(2) returns \"b\" and advances to the end, so UnreadByte restores 'b'. With zero as the argument, Peek returns an empty slice and preserves the prior unread opportunity, while Next clears it. With -1, both calls panic on the slice operation. After recovery, Peek has preserved the marker and Next has cleared it before the panic, so a subsequent UnreadByte has different results.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 359,
        "fragment": "b.lastRead = opInvalid"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Should Peek and Next document the valid range for n and the read-state effects of a zero or negative argument?"
    }
  ]
